{"id":"M2L1-E07","prompt":"An electricity provider uses these rates per unit: up to 100 at 0.10, 101–300 at 0.15, 301–500 at 0.20, and above 500 at 0.25. Complete or describe an ordered `if`/`elif`/`else` structure to select the rate, then state which rate applies at 100, 250, 500, and 501 units.","marks":5,"responseType":"branch design and boundary analysis","expectedAnswer":"Use if units<=100: rate=.10; elif units<=300: rate=.15; elif units<=500: rate=.20; else: rate=.25. The rates at 100, 250, 500, and 501 are .10, .15, .20, and .25. Ordering makes each later upper bound apply only after earlier ranges fail.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Uses ordered if/elif/else upper-bound structure correctly","points":1},{"criterion":"Selects 0.10 at exactly 100","points":1},{"criterion":"Selects 0.15 at 250","points":1},{"criterion":"Selects 0.20 at exactly 500","points":1},{"criterion":"Selects 0.25 at 501 and explains residual branch/order","points":1}],"source":"PDF pp. 36, 39; 2_making_decisions.md, electricity bill","operation":"design a branch chain and test boundary values"},
